 ## Overview
-Lighting and Appearance Models modify the standard NeRF architecture to explicitly represent illumination and material properties, enabling relighting and handling varying lighting conditions. Unlike standard NeRF, which learns view-dependent color implicitly via an MLP, these models separate albedo from illumination. They differ from siblings like [NeRF in the Wild (NeRF-W)](11995-nerf-in-the-wild-nerf-w.md) by explicitly modeling illumination rather than relying on appearance embeddings, and from [Neural Pre-Integrated Lighting (Neural-PIL)](13783-neural-pre-integrated-lighting-neural-pil.md) by using global bases rather than pre-integrated local lighting.
+Lighting and Appearance Models modify the standard NeRF architecture to explicitly represent, decompose, or stylize illumination and material properties, addressing the standard formulation's lack of semantic lighting control and its inability to handle varying lighting conditions or artistic styles [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md]. This family distinguishes itself by replacing or augmenting the view-dependent color MLP with explicit lighting bases, pre-integrated neural queries, or decoupled stylization networks, rather than relying solely on appearance embeddings [14428-sparse-view-and-few-shot-optimization.md].
 
 ## Shared principles
